<section class="sub" id="g-context">
<div class="callout quotecall">
<h4>The opportunity ahead</h4>
<p>Santa Fe's values have held up well since 1999 — what's changed is the opportunity to pair them with clear implementation. The active <a href="https://santafeforward.org/learn-more">Santa Fe Forward</a> update (targeting a 2050 horizon) is designed to do exactly that: naming the specific areas where the city will concentrate its funding, and giving specific direction for how goals and objectives get carried out.</p>
<p>An implementation framework like that is what lets a future council, staff member, or resident check the plan's progress against its own commitments.</p>
<p class="muted" style="color:var(--ink-muted); font-size:0.88rem; margin-top:12px;">Sources: <a href="https://www.santafenewmexican.com/news/business/city-seeks-feedback-volunteers-for-first-general-plan-update-in-25-years/article_d0df34a3-c9df-463c-b819-d2d21e5539ce.html">Santa Fe New Mexican</a> · <a href="https://santafeforward.org/learn-more">Santa Fe Forward</a> · 1999 plan chapters: <a href="https://www.santafenm.gov/document_center/document/1705">Themes</a>, <a href="https://www.santafenm.gov/document_center/document/1707">Land Use</a>, <a href="https://www.santafenm.gov/document_center/document/1709">City Character</a></p>
</div>
<p class="section-note">That opportunity is what separates the sharpest plans below from softer ones: not the vision language itself — most cities write similarly warm vision paragraphs — but whether every goal is paired with a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">named, numbered policy</strong>, a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">measurable target</strong>, and a <strong style="color:var(--ink);">stated implementation mechanism</strong> a future council or citizen can check.</p>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="g-plans">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">THE TWENTY PLANS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">City by city, in their own words</h2>
</div>
<div class="card-grid">
<article class="card" id="glg-minneapolis">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/minneapolis/">Minneapolis</a> <span class="place">Minneapolis 2040 Comprehensive Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2018, effective 2020 · horizon 2040</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Done right, growth can help our city become a healthy, sustainable, and thriving place for all."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal 9 — Complete Neighborhoods</b>"In 2040, all Minneapolis residents will have access to employment, retail services, healthy food, parks, and other daily needs via walking, biking, and public transit."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 6, action step</b>"Eliminate the requirement for off-street parking minimums throughout the city."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 58 — Business Districts and Corridors</b>"Support thriving business districts and corridors that build on cultural assets and serve the needs of Minneapolis residents."</p>
</div>
<p>~100 numbered policies, each a one-sentence directive plus numbered action steps naming real regulatory levers. Twelve future land-use categories map one-to-one onto fourteen "Built Form Districts" carrying actual height/FAR/setback standards.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> the goal format "In [year], [outcome] via [named mechanisms]"; numbering every policy for trackability; a visible policy-to-zoning-district crosswalk.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://minneapolis2040.com/media/2019/pdf_minneapolis2040-just-policy-document-updated-june-2024.pdf">Minneapolis 2040 policy document ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-austin">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/austin/">Austin</a> <span class="place">Imagine Austin Comprehensive Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2012 · horizon 2039 · mid full update</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"As it approaches its 200th anniversary, Austin is a beacon of sustainability, social equality, and economic opportunity... where the necessities of life are affordable and accessible to all."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Core concept</b>"The core concepts of Imagine Austin – complete communities and compact, connected centers – are two sides of the same coin."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Growth Concept Map</b>"The Growth Concept Map directs new and redevelopment to focused centers of varying sizes."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Named centers</b>48 specific, geocoded centers typed as Regional/Town/Neighborhood/Job Centers — e.g. "Mueller Station Town Center," "Plaza Saltillo Neighborhood Center."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Legal teeth</b>"Future zoning decisions may not disregard the comprehensive plan and must be made in accordance with the comprehensive plan."</p>
</div>
<p>The vision is translated into a literal map of 48 named, located centers linked by corridors chosen against explicit, checkable criteria — plus eight named "Priority Programs" that are the actual work programs (one became the CodeNEXT/land-code rewrite).</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> a single growth-concept map that names and geographically fixes each center/corridor; explicit criteria for what counts as a "corridor"; a short numbered list of priority implementation programs.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://services.austintexas.gov/edims/document.cfm?id=165140">Imagine Austin, full plan ↗</a> <a href="https://data.austintexas.gov/City-Government/Imagine-Austin-Comprehensive-Plan-Web-Full-PDF/ncss-muy9">Open Data listing ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-tacoma">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/tacoma/">Tacoma</a> <span class="place">One Tacoma + Home in Tacoma</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Rolling since 2015 · updated Jun 2025 · horizon 2044/2050</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Every Tacoma resident is a safe and short walk, roll, bus, train, or bike ride away from daily essentials and community amenities, such as groceries, schools, parks, and healthcare."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal GS-5</b>"Tacoma's growth is focused in a citywide network of transit-connected centers that anchor 15-minute neighborhoods providing nodes of activity and access to housing, employment, and services."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Density standard, Exhibit 18</b>"Regional Growth Centers are expected to plan for land use that accommodates at least 45 activity units (population and jobs) per gross acre."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy CN-2.7</b>"Introduce a system of compact blocks (300-400 feet in block face length) where opportunities to do so are feasible."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy H-1.2 / Section 3.1</b>"Middle housing types, such as townhomes, 2-3-4-plexes, and cottage housing are allowed across most all residential districts in Tacoma," against a stated need of "42,865 units... by 2044."</p>
</div>
<p>A tiered center hierarchy where each tier carries a hard numeric intensity target, not just a label; "walkability" becomes a testable standard (15-minute access, 300–400 ft block faces); housing policy is pinned to an actual production number.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> a mapped center hierarchy with a distinct numeric target per tier; a measurable "complete neighborhood" walk-time test; citywide default legalization of middle-housing types tied to a production number.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://cms.tacoma.gov/Planning/">Tacoma Planning documents ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-missoula">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/missoula/">Missoula</a> <span class="place">Our Missoula 2045 Land Use Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted Dec 2024 · horizon 2045</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision/values</b>"We believe that in order to achieve a healthy community and environment we must increase housing availability and access at all levels in all neighborhoods, enhance resiliency and adaptability, emphasize connectivity..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Centers/corridors</b>"Unlock the growth potential for mixed-use centers and corridors in order to allow more people to live within walking distance of their daily needs."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Housing distribution</b>Calls on all neighborhoods to "take on their fair share" of the city's housing needs.</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Concrete mechanism</b>Replaces Euclidean zoning with 12 "place types" and 8 "street types"; every residential place type permits a minimum of two units per parcel; a stated need of 22,000–27,500 new units by 2045; over 150 discrete implementation actions tied to code amendments.</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> the vision/values chapter itself reads fairly generic — the specificity lives in the land-use strategy and implementation chapters, not the opening vision. A plan can afford a warmer, less clinical vision statement as long as what follows is this concrete. Missoula followed through with a new Unified Development Code within about a year.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow (near-identical peer city):</b> a place-type/street-type system instead of a vague future-land-use map; a numbered implementation-action table tied to code sections; a minimum-density floor stated as an enforceable number.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.engagemissoula.com/growth-policy-update/documents">Full plan + appendices ↗</a> <a href="https://ourmissoula.konveio.com/our-missoula-2045-land-use-plan">Plan viewer ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-somerville">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/somerville/">Somerville</a> <span class="place">SomerVision 2040</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">2012, updated Oct 2021 · horizon 2040</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"SomerVision is an easy-to-use guide for future growth and development in the City, providing a policy framework for other planning efforts including neighborhood plans and capital plans."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Headline targets (public dashboard)</b>"30,000 new jobs" · "6,000 new housing units – 1,200 permanently affordable" · "125 new acres of publicly-accessible open space" · "85% of new development in transformative areas."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal VI</b>"Transform key opportunity areas, such as Assembly Square, Inner Belt, Brickbottom, Boynton Yards and the southeastern portion of Union Square into dynamic, mixed-use and transit-oriented districts..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Fiscal lever</b>Tiered inclusionary zoning (20% affordable for 18+ unit projects) plus a housing linkage fee of "$10 per square foot" on large commercial development.</p>
</div>
<p>Nearly every goal pairs a countable target with a named geography — not "encourage transit-oriented development" but "85% of new development in transformative areas," where the areas are named on a map. Progress is tracked on a public dashboard.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> pick a handful of numeric, trackable 20-year targets; name the actual places growth is meant to go; attach a real fiscal or zoning lever to each target.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="http://www.somervision2040.com/wp-content/uploads/sites/3/2021/10/SomerVision-2040-Adopted.pdf">SomerVision 2040, adopted plan ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-charlotte">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/charlotte/">Charlotte</a> <span class="place">Charlotte Future 2040</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted Jun 2021 · horizon 2040 → 2023 UDO</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Charlotte is America's Queen City, opening her arms to a diverse and inclusive community of residents, businesses, and visitors alike; a safe family-oriented city where people work together to help everyone thrive."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal 1 — 10-Minute Neighborhoods</b>"All Charlotte households should have access to essential amenities, goods, and services within a comfortable, tree-shaded 10-minute walk, bike, or transit trip by 2040," measured against nine named amenities.</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Place Types → code linkage</b>"These Place Types will in turn provide the policy-level guidance that will inform the City's Unified Development Ordinance (UDO)... Each Place Type will correspond with multiple zoning districts... on items such as height, lot size, setbacks, adjacencies, and allowed uses."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal 2, Policy 1 (missing middle)</b>"Allow single-family, duplex, and triplex housing units, as well as small footprint homes and accessory dwelling units (ADUs), in neighborhood Place Types."</p>
</div>
<p>The plan states outright, in its own text, that its Place Types will map to future zoning districts — the plan-to-code linkage is written as a commitment, not left implicit. "10-Minute Neighborhood" is dated, quantified, and publicly legible.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> state explicitly that land-use designations will map one-to-one onto future LDC zoning districts; adopt a single testable walkability standard as the plan's signature commitment.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://cltfuture2040.com">cltfuture2040.com ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-boulder">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/boulder/">Boulder</a> <span class="place">Boulder Valley Comprehensive Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">2020 update, adopted 2021 · ~25-year horizon</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"The Boulder Valley community honors its history and legacy of planning for a livable community surrounded by open space and rural lands while striving together to create and preserve a truly special place that is sustainable, resilient, equitable and inclusive..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 2.03</b>Development should "take advantage of existing urban services, and avoid, insofar as possible, patterns of leapfrog, noncontiguous, scattered development."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 2.19, Neighborhood Centers</b>"Neighborhood centers contribute to a sense of place and the achievement of walkable (15-minute) places with a mix of uses and range of services."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Historic commitment</b>"An inclusionary zoning ordinance was adopted, requiring 20 percent of new residential development in the city to be permanently affordable."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> Boulder's centers/density language leans more "will encourage... in appropriate locations" than a hard mandate — reflecting a growth-boundary tradition focused more on limiting growth than channeling it into dense, walkable form. A useful example of why pairing "encourage" language with firmer standards strengthens even a well-organized, numbered plan. A full new BVCP update was just adopted Aug 21, 2026; its text isn't public yet.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> the numbered-policy-tied-to-a-map format, the specific 20% inclusionary number, and the "15-minute" metric — while writing Santa Fe's center/density policies as clearer mandates than Boulder's "encourage" language.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://bouldercolorado.gov/sites/default/files/2021-06/bvcp2020finalplan.pdf">2020 BVCP final plan ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-flagstaff">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/flagstaff/">Flagstaff</a> <span class="place">Flagstaff Regional Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">2030 plan (2014) → 2045 plan (May 2026)</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LU.2.1 (2030)</b>"Design new neighborhoods that embody the characteristics of Flagstaff's favorite neighborhoods – that is, with a mix of uses, a variety of housing types and densities."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Regional Plan 2045, Policy DP.2</b>"Support development and redevelopment with a diversity of housing types by expanding opportunities for missing middle housing."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Regional Plan 2045, Policy WR.4</b>"Provide sustainable and reliable water and sewer services within the urban growth boundary" — paired with: "Do not extend water and sewer services beyond the urban growth boundary unless an analysis shows the service can be provided without impacting the availability of water to property owners already within the boundary."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Zoning Code §10-00.060</b>Six named transect zones from T1 (rural preserve) to T6 (downtown core), with T5 defined as main-street commercial corridors — "character area" language translated directly into a mapped, buildable zoning ordinance.</p>
</div>
<p><strong>Likely the single closest national peer to Santa Fe</strong> — similar population, historic downtown, university presence, high-desert setting, severe housing-cost burden, heavy tourism. Its 2045 plan was approved by voters in May 2026, an unusually fresh comparable.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> write every land-use goal as a short, numbered policy a future zoning amendment can cite by number; pair the plan with a transect/character-area zoning framework so "compact, walkable, historic-compatible" becomes mapped, enforceable rules.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.flagstaff.az.gov">flagstaff.az.gov ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-santabarbara">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/santabarbara/">Santa Barbara</a> <span class="place">General Plan / Housing Element</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Core plan 2011 · Housing Element 2023–2031</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>El Pueblo Viejo historic district</b>"The purpose of El Pueblo Viejo is to preserve and enhance the unique heritage and architectural character of the central area of the city... while allowing reasonable development."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Average Unit-Size Density Incentive Program</b>"Encourage smaller, more affordable units through established unit sizes, while allowing flexibility for larger units, which help subsidize the cost of the smaller units." Target unit size: "approximately 1,000 square feet, sufficient to accommodate two bedrooms."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Housing Element, Program HE-6</b>"The City shall review objective design standards to ensure they do not unnecessarily constrain housing production."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>AUD Program (2013)</b>Allows "increased densities (up to 63 units/acre)" in exchange for the unit-size formula above.</p>
</div>
<p>The closest comparable among historic, tourism-economy, strict-design-review cities. Historic preservation is explicitly bounded ("while allowing reasonable development"), not an unqualified trump card.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> adopt one concrete numeric mechanism (a unit-size-based density formula, or similar by-right envelope rule) that operationalizes the historic-character/housing tradeoff inside the existing height/FAR envelope, plus a standing commitment to audit whether design standards constrain housing production.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://santabarbaraca.gov/HousingElement">Housing Element Implementation ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-portland">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/portland/">Portland</a> <span class="place">Portland 2035 Comprehensive Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2016 · horizon 2035</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Portland is a prosperous, healthy, equitable and resilient city where everyone has access to opportunity and is engaged in shaping decisions that affect their lives."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 3.2 — Growth and stability</b>"Direct the majority of growth and change to centers, corridors, and transit station areas, allowing the continuation of the scale and characteristics of Portland's residential neighborhoods."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 3.18</b>"Design centers to be compact, safe, attractive, and accessible places, where the street environment makes access by transit, walking, biking, and mobility devices... safe and attractive for people of all ages and abilities."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Chapter 3 narrative</b>"This network of well-functioning centers and corridors will be critical to achieve the Portland Plan goals to make it possible for most Portlanders (80 percent) to live in complete neighborhoods by 2035."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 3.36</b>Calls for housing capacity "to accommodate 3,500 households" within a half-mile walk of designated Neighborhood Centers.</p>
</div>
<p>This is the umbrella plan the Residential Infill Project implements. It names *where* growth goes and explicitly trades that off against protecting existing neighborhood scale elsewhere, backed by numeric capacity targets ("80% in complete neighborhoods," "3,500 households").</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> pair every centers/corridors goal with a numeric capacity or mode-share target; state the growth-direction trade-off explicitly rather than leaving it implicit.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.portland.gov/bps/planning/comp-plan-2035">Portland 2035 Comprehensive Plan ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-sacramento">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/sacramento/">Sacramento</a> <span class="place">Sacramento 2040 General Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted Feb 2024 · horizon 2040</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"In 2040, the City of Sacramento will be a national model of sustainable, equitable growth and community development."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal LUP-4</b>"Walkable, transit-oriented centers and corridors that concentrate new jobs, housing, and entertainment opportunities to support frequent, reliable transit service."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LUP-4.1</b>"The City shall encourage increased residential and commercial development intensity within ½-mile of existing high-frequency bus stops and existing and planned light rail... stations."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LUP-6.3 — Missing Middle</b>Names the actual typologies: "duplexes, triplexes, fourplexes, cottage/bungalow courts, neighborhood-scale multi-unit buildings" sitting "in the middle of a spectrum between detached single-unit homes and mid-rise apartment buildings."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> policies attach real numeric triggers (½-mile radius, named housing types) but the plan sets intensity/location targets without guaranteeing approval speed, financing, or infrastructure funding — consistent with local reporting that construction is lagging well behind the legalization.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> name exact housing types and a geographic trigger (distance-to-transit) in policy text — but pair that language with the implementation mechanisms Sacramento's plan itself lacks.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.cityofsacramento.gov/content/dam/portal/cdd/Planning/adopted-2040-general-plan/2040%20GP_2-03_Land%20Use%20and%20Placemaking_Adopted.pdf">Land Use &amp; Placemaking Element ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-berkeley">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/berkeley/">Berkeley</a> <span class="place">Berkeley General Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2002 · amended through 2025</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision framing</b>"Berkeley represents an intricate and delicate balance that is constantly undergoing subtle changes in its physical and social fabric."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Goal 1</b>"Preserve Berkeley's unique character and quality of life."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LU-26 / LU-27</b>Names actual districts rather than generic categories: "Maintain and improve Neighborhood Commercial areas, such as Elmwood, Solano, and North Shattuck, as pedestrian-friendly, visually attractive areas..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LU-23</b>"Encourage and maintain zoning that allows greater commercial and residential density and reduced residential parking requirements in areas with above-average transit service such as Downtown Berkeley."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> this reads as typical early-2000s comp-plan language — soft, "character and balance" framing at the vision level. Notably, "missing middle" and the 2023 single-family-zoning repeal appear nowhere in the General Plan text itself: Berkeley made that change as a standalone zoning ordinance rather than a General Plan amendment, so there's a clear opportunity to bring the plan's own housing-type language up to date.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> naming specific corridors/districts in policy text makes it enforceable — and updating the plan alongside a major zoning reform keeps that reform backed by the comprehensive plan, not just the zoning code.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://berkeleyca.gov/sites/default/files/documents/02_Land%20Use%20Element-FINAL.pdf">Berkeley Land Use Element ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-cincinnati">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/cincinnati/">Cincinnati</a> <span class="place">Plan Cincinnati</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2012 · no fixed horizon</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"An unapologetic drive to create and sustain a thriving inclusive urban community, where engaged people and memorable places are paramount... where local pride and confidence are contagious."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Neighborhood centers concept</b>"Our neighborhoods are structured around centers of activity that contain all of the amenities that we need to go about our daily life. We will focus our development on these centers of activity..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>"Live" initiative strategy</b>"Become more walkable" and "Provide a full spectrum of housing options, and improve housing quality and affordability."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> vivid and specific about physical form (walkable, mixed-use centers), with room to strengthen the goal/strategy layer ("Become more walkable," "Strengthen our magnetic city") with numeric targets, timelines, or "shall" language. Pairing that vision with specific, agreed-upon zoning detail up front — before a Connected-Communities-style translation into by-right density arrives — is what earns lasting public buy-in.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> the strong sensory/form-based vision language is worth emulating, but pair it with genuinely measurable, geographically-specific policies in the plan itself so zoning implementation isn't the first time specifics appear.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.cincinnati-oh.gov/planning/plan-cincinnati/">Plan Cincinnati ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-columbus">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/columbus/">Columbus</a> <span class="place">Columbus Growth Strategy</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted Jan 2026 · horizon 2045</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Columbus will embrace and guide growth to build a future that provides opportunities for all residents."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Density standard, Mixed Use 1</b>"Guideline: 24 units/acre OR 72 bedrooms/acre — Heights of 3–4 stories."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Density standard, Mixed Use 3</b>"Guideline: Unlimited units/acre — Heights of 8+ stories."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Guiding Principle 1a</b>"Mixed-use and highest density residential development should be focused along primary corridors, at established commercial centers, and at locations recommended for mixed-use development with access to... transit."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Housing variety</b>Names "missing middle housing" explicitly alongside "accessory dwelling units (ADUs), age-friendly housing, and universally designed housing" as a stated priority for all neighborhoods.</p>
</div>
<p>The vision line is generic, but it's immediately anchored to concrete, numeric standards — units/acre <em>and</em> bedrooms/acre thresholds paired with story-height ranges per tier — exactly the testable language a generic "encourage appropriate density" policy lacks.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> pair each land-use category with an explicit density-and-height table; state missing-middle/ADU support as a named priority rather than folding it into generic "housing diversity" language.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.columbus.gov/files/sharedassets/city/v/1/bzs/archived/planning-document-library/columbus-growth-strategy.pdf">Columbus Growth Strategy (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card clay" id="glg-gainesville">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/gainesville/">Gainesville</a> <span class="place">Comprehensive Plan, Future Land Use Element</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Currently adopted FLUE · ImagineGNV 2050 drafted Apr 2026</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Goal 1</b>"Improve the quality of life and achieve a superior, sustainable development pattern in the city by creating and maintaining choices in housing... within walking distance of important destinations."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Mixed-Use Low-Intensity category</b>Permits "small-lot single-family houses, duplex houses, townhouses (attached housing), accessory dwelling units, group homes, multi-family housing" — language still in the plan today, in 2026.</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Why this one matters most:</b> the 2022 reform and its 2023 reversal both lived in the Land Development Code and zoning-district text — not in the comprehensive plan, whose permissive categories were untouched by either change. The lesson: an allowance anchored in comp-plan policy (which in Florida requires a state transmittal/review process) carries the kind of durability friction that helps a reform last.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow — this is the case for doing GP + LDC together:</b> an upzoning that never touches the comprehensive plan is exactly as durable as the next election. Anchor missing-middle allowances in adopted comp-plan policy, not zoning-code text alone.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.gainesvillefl.gov/files/assets/public/v/1/sustainable-development/planning-department/documents/currently-adopted-comp-plan/goals-objectives-and-policies-future-land-use.pdf">Future Land Use Element (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card clay" id="glg-arlington">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/arlington/">Arlington County</a> <span class="place">Comprehensive Plan / General Land Use Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">GLUP est. 1960 · current edition Jan 2023</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"Arlington will be a diverse and inclusive world-class urban community with secure, attractive residential and commercial neighborhoods..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Corridors/centers goal</b>"Concentrate high-density development within Metro Station Areas in the Rosslyn-Ballston and Route 1/Richmond Highway Metrorail Transit Corridors."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>EHO/GLUP amendment, Section 5.4 (2023)</b>Adopted the <em>same day</em> as the zoning ordinance changes, "adds language to the GLUP map to include accessory dwelling units, and EHO uses in areas planned for 'Low' Residential uses."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Why this one matters most:</b> unlike Gainesville, Arlington's "Expanded Housing Options" was <em>not</em> a zoning-only change — the County amended both the GLUP and the Zoning Ordinance the same day. A 2024 circuit court raised narrow <em>procedural</em> questions unrelated to comp-plan consistency (an infrastructure study, a public notice, a delegation-of-authority detail), and the policy was reinstated on appeal in June 2025. The takeaway: a comp-plan anchor and a carefully documented adoption <em>process</em> reinforce each other.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> amend the General Plan and the Land Development Code together (as Arlington did) — but also paper-trail every procedural step (impact studies, public notice language, delegation of authority) as carefully as the policy substance.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.arlingtonva.us/files/sharedassets/public/projects/documents/comprehensive-plan/comp-plan_essential-guide-2023.pdf">Comprehensive Plan Essential Guide (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card clay" id="glg-alexandria">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/alexandria/">Alexandria</a> <span class="place">Master Plan (citywide chapters)</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Land Use 1992 · Zoning for Housing amendment Nov 2023</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Original density-cap policy (pre-2023)</b>"Areas of the city currently zoned residential should remain zoned for residential use at no higher than their current density."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Amended text, MPA #2023-00005</b>Same clause with the density cap struck: "Areas of the City currently zoned residential should remain zoned for residential use" — the cap itself removed from the Master Plan, not just the zoning code.</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Equity mandate added</b>"Ensure race and social equity is 'incorporated and centered in all planning'... including... all references to preserving and protecting neighborhoods and character."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Measurable target</b>"The City shall uphold the commitment contained in Resolution 830 to maintain 1150 publicly-assisted housing units."</p>
</div>
<p>"Zoning for Housing" was formally grounded in an amended Master Plan (a struck density-cap clause, a new equity mandate) enacted alongside the zoning text amendment — giving the reform an explicit, adopted policy record for courts to point to as legislative intent. A judge dismissed the legal challenge in Nov. 2025.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> when removing a density cap or upzoning, amend the actual General Plan text — not just zoning code — and pair the change with an explicit, citable finding or policy statement it implements.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://www.alexandriava.gov/sites/default/files/2024-01/mpa202300005.pdf">Master Plan Amendment #2023-00005 (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-bend">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/bend/">Bend</a> <span class="place">Bend Comprehensive Plan</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">1981, reformatted 2016 · 20-yr UGB horizon</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Goal, Ch. 11</b>"Create new walkable, mixed use and complete communities by leveraging and complementing land use patterns inside the existing boundary..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 11-32</b>"The City will support re-designation of suitable areas that are within a ¼ mile walk to transit corridors from a lower density designation to a higher density designation."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy 11-51 / Table 11-1</b>Master plans require minimum density standards from <b>2.0 dwellings/gross acre</b> (Urban Low Density) up to <b>21.7 dwellings/gross acre</b> (Urban High Density), plus a required housing-type mix.</p>
</div>
<p>The vision statement is soft civic-pride language, but Chapter 11 becomes concrete fast: named geographies (Central Core, Opportunity Areas, transit corridors), a numeric density floor per designation, and a bright-line spatial test (¼-mile walk to transit) rather than "encourage density near transit."</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> keep an aspirational vision paragraph, but follow it immediately with a typology table — Urban Center / Corridor / Neighborhood Center — each carrying a minimum density number and a walk-distance trigger for upzoning.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://bendoregon.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/Bend-Comprehensive-Plan-2016.pdf">Bend Comprehensive Plan (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-spokane">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/spokane/">Spokane</a> <span class="place">Spokane Comprehensive Plan ("Shaping Spokane")</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Adopted 2001 · land use ch. amended 2020</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Land use vision</b>"Growth will be managed to allow a mix of land uses that fit, support, and enhance Spokane's neighborhoods, protect the environment, and sustain the downtown area..."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LU 1.4</b>"Direct new higher density residential uses to Centers and Corridors designated on the Land Use Plan Map."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Table LU-1</b>Sets numeric mandatory use-mix targets per center tier — Neighborhood Centers: 10% public / 20% commercial / 40% higher-density housing.</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Policy LU 3.6 (compact/missing-middle)</b>Defines "compact housing" by name — "townhouses, accessory dwelling units (granny flats), live-work housing, triplexes, zero-lot line, starter, small-lot, and row houses" — allowed "in all neighborhoods."</p>
</div>
<p>The tightest plan-to-code linkage in this set: Policy LU 3.6 names the exact housing typologies the 2023 middle-housing ordinance then legalized citywide, and Table LU-1 sets numeric density/use-mix targets rather than vague "encourage a mix" language. One vision sentence → one goal → numbered policies with quantified thresholds and named housing forms, almost one-to-one onto zoning standards.</p>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow:</b> pair each vision statement with 1-2 goals, then policies naming specific building/lot typologies plus at least one quantitative table, so the zoning code has an unambiguous policy citation to implement.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://static.spokanecity.org/documents/shapingspokane/comprehensive-plan/chapter-3-land-use-v4.pdf">Shaping Spokane, Ch. 3 Land Use (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
<article class="card" id="glg-southbend">
<div class="card-top">
<h3><a href="/cities/southbend/">South Bend</a> <span class="place">South Bend 2045 Roadmap + Infill Catalog</span></h3>
<span class="tagline">Roadmap 2023, not yet adopted · Infill Catalog 2022</span>
</div>
<div class="quote-stack">
<p class="quote"><b>Vision</b>"A Shared Vision for South Bend 2045" — connecting "people to each other, to place and to opportunity."</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Growth policy</b>Prioritize "density and infill within the city limits" over outward expansion.</p>
<p class="quote"><b>Catalog purpose</b>"The City of South Bend seeks to support neighborhood infill... by offering a set of pre-approved building types," addressing "a growing mismatch between the available housing stock of large single-family homes and a shift in demographic trends towards smaller households that prefer walkable neighborhoods."</p>
</div>
<div class="note"><b>Honest note:</b> South Bend doesn't yet have a fully adopted new comprehensive plan — the 2045 process is still underway. Its most actionable artifact is the infill catalog, a literal buildable deliverable rather than plan language. And the parallel to Santa Fe is imperfect: South Bend built this to fight population loss and cheap land by making infill fast and free to design, while Santa Fe's binding constraint is the opposite — high land/construction costs plus historic-district review.</div>
<p class="borrow"><b>Borrow the model, adapt the target:</b> a ready-to-permit pattern book scaled to context turns "increase housing diversity" into something a builder can pull a permit against — a Santa Fe version would need to solve for cost and historic-preservation compatibility, not just permitting speed.</p>
<div class="src-row"><a href="https://southbendin.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/South-Bend-2045-Plan-ROADMAP.pdf">South Bend 2045 Roadmap (PDF) ↗</a></div>
</article>
